TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Sun-synchronous inclination at 600 km stated as 87.79 degrees, which is prograde and contradicts the plotted value (about 97.8 degrees) and the 'retrograde' claim."},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"Semi-major axis of 7171 km is wrong; 6371 + 600 gives 6971 km."},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"RAAN is said to indicate the launch facility location, which is conceptually incorrect."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou latitude given as 45.05 N; it is about 5.2 N. The launch-window reasoning based on this value is also confused."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claimed average contact of almost twenty minutes conflicts with the table mean of 580.786 s (about 9.7 min)."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications (access table)","issue":"Access 13 is duplicated, and the text says fifteen periods but only fourteen are listed; total duration does not match the rows."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims the descending pass is constantly sunlit; a 600 km SSO has eclipse seasons, and this is not analysed."},{"where":"Orbit / Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"States only manoeuvre is at EOL while also stating the satellite has no thrusters, so no EOL manoeuvre is possible as described."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"No decay or lifetime analysis supports the 5-year duration or the claim that uncontrolled re-entry is passive and sufficient at 600 km."},{"where":"Executive Summary","issue":"Claims perturbation calculations are included, but none are presented."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is cited despite being explicitly unacceptable; tables cite references 49 to 74 that do not appear in the bibliography."}],"overall":"The report covers the required mission segments with STK support, but significant technical errors undermine it: wrong inclination contradicting the plot, wrong semi-major axis, wrong launch latitude and an overstated contact claim. EOL is incomplete, operators are unidentified, and citations are inconsistent with Wikipedia used. Presentation has truncated sentences and a wrong date.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"","mark":0.0,"scale":10.0,"points":0.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"","evidence":[]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"STK screenshots and an access table give some evidence, and the required topics are touched on. However, the 87.79 degree inclination contradicts the plot (about 97.8 degrees) and the 'retrograde' claim. Semi-major axis is wrong (6971 km, not 7171 km). The Kourou latitude is wrong. The 'twenty minute' contact claim contradicts the table mean of about 581 s. Manoeuvres, operations, and EOL are inconsistent and thinly supported, and perturbation and hand calculations are absent.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Required to achieve an altitude of 600km while remaining sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Earth radius of 6371km plus a proposed altitude of 600km as previously explained."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"it is situated very close to the equator, with a latitude of"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"Thus, abandoning the satellite at its EOL is not environmentally or socially responsible."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":4.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises each segment and acknowledges the Vega E launch risk. However, it is generic, draws no quantitative conclusions from the STK results, and overclaims that the mission will prevent bleaching. It restates the report rather than reaching specific, evidence-based conclusions.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The initial problem of not catching coral bleaching events in time can be minimised, if not solved"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear headings, an executive summary and a contents page give a reasonable structure. However, there are truncated sentences, typos, informal wording, and an incorrect date on the title page. Tables and images are not numbered. Citations are inconsistent: tables cite refs 49 to 74, which are absent, Wikipedia is used, and reference entries are garbled.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design / Orbit","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"},{"where":"Title page","quote":"AERO9500 - T3 2020"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"a wiki sorted directory"},{"where":"Launch tables","quote":"Small Satellites Mission Service (SSMS) piggyback mission."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[10 d."}]}]}
